{#
   THE DATA TABLE — one linear-list shape, the way `_catalogue` is one grid shape.

   S59 put seven public catalogues on two shared templates. This is its twin for
   the other half of the product: **25 hand-rolled tables across 13 different
   class names**, each with its own `<thead>`, its own empty row, its own
   `colspan`, and — in twelve cases — its own stylesheet block.

   Parameters:

     columns  a list of `{label: '…'}`. `label` is **already translated**, the
              same contract `_catalogue` uses; an empty label is a legitimate
              header (the thumbnail column). Optional per column:
                numeric: true   right-aligns the column
                width:   '…'    a CSS width for that column
                class:   '…'    extra classes for the `<th>`. ⚠️ Added in S84
                                for the trailing actions column, whose `<td>`s
                                right-align while its `<th>` did not — the shell
                                writes the header and the caller writes the
                                cells, so with no way to say it the two halves
                                of one column could not agree. It is a class
                                string and nothing else; the shell still knows
                                nothing about what a column means.
     rows     the collection, used **only** to decide empty-or-not. The caller
              iterates it itself.
     empty    the sentence shown when `rows` is empty.

   Blocks:

     rows         the `<tr>`s. The caller writes its own `{% for %}` — see below.
     empty_extra  optional: something after the empty sentence, e.g. a "create
                  the first one" link.

   ⚠️ **The caller owns the loop, exactly as `_catalogue` hands its `cards` block
   the whole grid.** The tidier-looking alternative — this shell running the
   `{% for %}` and calling a `row` block inside it — relies on the loop variable
   reaching a block defined in another template. That may well work, but there is
   **no instance of it anywhere in this codebase** to prove it, and with
   `strict_variables: true` being wrong is not a cosmetic bug: it is a 500 on
   every admin list at once. Matching the pattern that has already survived eight
   surfaces costs the caller three lines and cannot fail that way.

   ⚠️ **`colspan` is computed here and must never be written by a caller.** All
   25 originals hand-counted it (`<td colspan="5">`), and a hand-counted colspan
   is wrong the moment a column is added — silently, because the only way to see
   it is to empty the table, which nobody does on purpose.

   ⚠️ **This template computes nothing else.** Same rule as `_catalogue_card`: it
   does not know what a place or a loan is, and it must not learn. The caller
   decides every word and every cell. An `{% if %}` on entity type here ends the
   exercise.

   ⚠️ **The wrapper is not optional.** `.admin-table-wrap` is what makes a table
   survive a narrow screen — a `<table>` is the one element that cannot be made
   to reflow — and emitting it here is how S52's mobile work stops being 25
   separate jobs.
#}
{# `pin_actions: true` — the last column becomes the fixed-width sticky action
   column (S85). ⚠️ Opt-in, never automatic on `:last-child`: a list whose last
   column is data would get a sticky data column and a shadow in the middle of
   nowhere. Pass it wherever the last column really is the verb. #}
<div class="admin-table-wrap">
    <table class="admin-table{{ pin_actions|default(false) ? ' has-pinned-actions' : '' }}">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                {% for column in columns %}
                    {%- set column_class = (column.numeric|default(false) ? 'is-numeric ' : '') ~ column.class|default('') -%}
                    <th{% if column_class|trim %} class="{{ column_class|trim }}"{% endif %}
                       {%- if column.width|default('') %} style="width: {{ column.width }}"{% endif %}>{{ column.label|default('') }}</th>
                {% endfor %}
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% if rows|default([])|length %}
                {% block rows %}{% endblock %}
            {% else %}
                <tr class="admin-table-empty">
                    <td colspan="{{ columns|length }}">
                        {{ empty|default('') }}
                        {% block empty_extra %}{% endblock %}
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endif %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
